{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789931429,"nonce":"tYVs1D1syxyGf3gKR41T1uT9U+w=","title":"Tuple unpacking from a block-valued tuple reads the wrong slot","message":"n, = \u003cexpr\u003e where the expression lowers to a block value (statements, then a one-field tuple struct) bound the name to 0: the first struct.unpack lowering produced its tuple as Self::block_value(pre, tuple, ty) and the caller's tuple_fields/hold read slot 0 of the held block as 0 instead of the field. t = \u003csame expr\u003e then n, = t was right, and one() returning a one-tuple was right, so the fault is in binding a tuple target directly to a block-valued struct (probably the single-field flatten path in SSA meeting a TypedExpression::Block). struct_unpack now hoists its prologue and returns the bare tuple, which sidesteps it; the general case is still broken and needs a reproducer built from the typed AST.","files":null}]}